00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:04.539
ran like this

00:01:04.539 --> 00:01:07.390
“I’m a vampire, I’m a vampire!”

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.240
and ate a live cockroach like this.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.990
By the way we just covered this insanely kinda
awesome movie on our movie podcast “Show

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.049
Me the Meaning”, we’re actually doing
a full month of Niolas Cage film.

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:25.030
So check out the links in the description
to join us on this journey into the CaGe abyss.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:28.869
Movies like Vampire’s Kiss provide some
clue as to why Nic Cage’s acting career

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:33.009
is one of the most disputed of any leading
Hollywood star.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:36.360
"
"The tortures of the damned!!"

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.920
Some people love the guy.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.020
Ethan Hawke calls him the only actor to evolve
the craft since Marlon Brando, while David

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.689
Lynch dubbed him the jazz musician of acting.Now
I know what you’re thinking.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:49.299
THIS Nic Cage?

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:55.899
“Not the bees!Not the bees!

00:01:55.899 --> 00:01:57.219
Ahhhh!”

00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:02.000
THIS Nic Cage?

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.579
The Nic Cage that’s motivation for this
scene was

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:09.230
“My whole motivation here was try to see
how big I could get my eyes, just to freak

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:10.470
her out.”

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.470
Yep.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:12.470
THAT Nic Cage

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.000
“Am I a BEEPING retard?

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Ahhh?!”

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.430
In recent years, Cage has become just as well
known for the ridiculous memes he inspires

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.800
as the roles he plays,

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.030
[Grunting]

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:26.080
leaving us movie-lovers at Wisecrack really
and truly befuddled.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.360
So today in this Wisecrack Edition, we’re
asking “Nicolas Cage…

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.680
Deep or Dumb?”

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.909
Let’s investigate.

00:02:31.909 --> 00:02:33.760
Who is Nic Cage anyway?

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.620
Nephew of director Francis Ford Coppola, Cage
is arguably one of the hardest working actors

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:44.239
in Hollywood, with over 73 films in his repertoire
. The guy’s also pretty bad with money,

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:48.650
which may have something to do with his apparent
inability to turn down any paying gig.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:54.080
He’s an unusual Hollywood leading man, having
appeared in stinkers, weirdly-great indie

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.110
films, big action films and everything in
between.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.250
His refusal to be typecast also makes him
difficult to wrap your head around - he’s

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.940
basically half leading man/half character
actor.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.769
To understand Nicolas Cage, and this

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:09.349
“T, U, V, X, Y, Z!”

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:13.909
we need to put him in the broader context
of film performance history.

00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:17.659
When the first silent narrative films started
being made, they drew largely from the most

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:22.440
popular performance entertainment of the time
- theatre, and particularly the genres of

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.459
melodrama and vaudeville.

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:29.060
Both forms called for large, sweeping gestures
that could be as easily understood in the

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.000
balcony as in the front row.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.390
This tradition was coupled with the limitations
of the silent film genre, which had zero verbal

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.610
communication other than this fancy typeset.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.299
Actors had only their faces and their bodies
to work with.

00:03:41.299 --> 00:03:45.610
That meant lots of wide eyes, ugly crying
and and berserk-o gestures.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:48.250
It wasn’t “realistic,” and it didn’t
try to be.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:53.319
Then, in the late 1920s, “talkie” films
with synchronized sound started becoming technologically

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:54.909
and commercially viable.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:59.870
Suddenly, actors were forced to rethink their
entire craft, a crisis best encapsulated by

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.370
this moment of Singing in the Rain, in which
a famous silent film actor clumsily attempts

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.750
to translate his craft into the new era of
sound.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:14.099
“I love you, I love you, I love you, love
you…”

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:16.049
“Somebody get paid for writing that dialogue?”

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:21.250
Change was slow, and movies of the 1930s typically
show stars expressing a heightened reality

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.590
that harkens back to their silent film roots.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:28.110
"You aint mad at me Min, are ya?"

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.820
"No, I ain't mad at ya"

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.860
Importantly, just a few years earlier, America
had been introduced to the groundbreaking

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.790
techniques of Constantine Stanislavsky, a
Russian acting coach whose ideas about realism

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.680
would eventually be turned into method acting.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.930
Basically, Stanislvaksy, and method acting,
attempt to help actors channel “emotional

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.080
truth” from within.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.680
“Hey Stella!!”

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.840
As these techniques became adapted from theatre
to film, actors began turning their characters

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:02.870
into fully-rounded people, and sometimes briefly
living as those people, like Daniel Day Lewis

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.730
who spent the entire production of My Left
Foot in a wheelchair.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:11.520
These antics were eventually packaged as publicity
gold which is why Leo almost froze to death

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.970
and Jared Leto mailed out dead pig heads.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:18.860
Anyway, method’s emphasis on realism, coupled
with the enhanced realism offered by sound

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.870
film, congealed in the 1940s and 50s, and
swept Hollywood.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:26.900
Actors like Marlon Brando became famous for
disappearing into a character.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.250
While there have certainly been ripples of
other directing and acting styles, such as

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.530
the dementedly-over-the-top, campy performances
seen in John Waters films.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:38.660
“Someone will pay with their life for their
grossly…”

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.160
“Mama, nobody since expects to live…”

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.370
There’s remained an overwhelming emphasis
on realism as the goal of acting for film.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.500
We typically award Oscars to actors who have
disappeared into their roles, frequently while

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.190
portraying real people.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:53.729
Which is all well and good.

00:05:53.729 --> 00:05:56.509
Except when we become dogmatic about it.

00:05:56.509 --> 00:06:00.600
Cinema scholar Carole Zucker argues that “the
idea that acting for film should adhere to

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:07.840
a standard of gestural and psychological verisimilitude
(ie realness/trueness) suggests a limited

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.710
vision of performance.”

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:13.840
Basically- what if the dominance of realism
in acting has left us with narrow vision of

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.630
what acting can be.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:20.699
Is the reason Nic Cage performances look so
weird because he falls outside this view?

00:06:20.699 --> 00:06:26.020
That’s the persuasive argument made by author
Lindsay Gibb, in National Treasure, her book-long

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.740
love letter to the actor.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:32.630
Gibs recounts the way Nic Cage originally
embraced method acting, even having two teeth

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.479
removed for his role as the injured veteran
in 1984’s Birdie.

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:38.860
But Cage quickly tired of the style.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.900
Thus began the truly Cage-ian search for a
form of acting that went… beyond.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:50.060
“Too late, Too late, Too late, Too late”

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:55.490
In 1990, he told American Film, “I wanted
to put back into acting a more surreal or

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:01.060
experimental style that wasn’t totally run
by literalism, which seemed like a dead end.”

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.970
Cage considers Vampire’s Kiss to have been
the “laboratory” for the new ideas he

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:09.449
was toying with, a low-stakes indie setting
where he could experiment, play and and recite

00:07:09.449 --> 00:07:10.449
his ABCs.

00:07:10.449 --> 00:07:13.030
“A, B, C, D, E, F, G”

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.139
Sorry, we really love that scene

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:16.440
“I never misfiled anything!”

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.830
In Vampire’s Kiss, we start to see the Nic
Cage we’ve come to know and meme.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:21.910
“There you are!”

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:27.580
He makes bold daring choices that take advantage
of all aspects of his physicality,including

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.660
some patently goofy facial expressions.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:34.770
While we’re accustomed to seeing some of
this broad-style acting in every era of screwball

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:39.760
comedy,Cage would go on to employ it fearlessly
whether he’s working in melodrama, action

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:40.760
or horror.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:41.760
"Glass or plastic?

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.760
Glass or plastic?

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:43.760
Glass or plastic?"

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.180
He is unapologetically performative.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:49.240
While that quality proves confusing for the
viewer in naturalistic films like Vampire’s

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:54.120
Kiss, it tends to work really well in movies
that push the limits of reality - movies set

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.710
in surreal worlds that can accommodate his
bold choices.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:01.759
Take Cage’s bombastic performance in Moonstruck,
which is fitting for a film

00:08:01.759 --> 00:08:04.340
preoccupied with the dramatics of opera.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:09.740
As Ronnie, the long-estranged brother of Teresa’s
fiancee Johnnie, Cage expresses a volatility

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.460
that is captivating to watch.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.930
“I lost my hand, I lost my bride.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:18.400
Johnny has his hand, Johnny has his bride.”

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.760
Over 30 years later, in Mandy, Cage gives
an unrestrained heavy metal performance only

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.200
appropriate for a LSD-fueled film featuring
a sword-boner.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:33.200
Similarly, in the dream world of David Lynch’s
Wild At Heart, Cage’s outrageous, brain-bashing,

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:36.010
Elvis-crooning Sailor feels right at home.

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:38.510
“This is a snakeskin jacket!

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:43.900
And for me it's a symbol of my individuality,
and my belief... in personal freedom.”

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:49.180
Cage’s dialed-up style can work, provided
the filmmaker and the other actors are in

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.180
on it.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:54.580
Cage’s outrageous antics lead many a critic
to deem him “out of control” or “over

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:55.580
the top.”

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.230
“DIE!!!”

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.260
[laughs]

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.290
[screams]

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:07.320
[gags]

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.350
[screams]

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:15.230
But understanding how he approaches his craft
kind of changes everything.

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:19.130
Cage is incredibly thoughtful and incredibly
well-versed in the craft of acting.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:24.540
Some of his most famously- ridiculous moments
are planned out, move by move by move.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.780
“BEEP you BEEP you BEEP you.”

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:35.640
Take the famous alphabet recitation - of which
Cage says every one of those moves, each hand

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.390
clap, hip thrust, pout and holler was carefully
thought and planned out.

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:45.000
At other times, Cage takes inspiration from
Kabuki, a Japanese avant-garde performance

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.260
art that combines acting, dancing and singing,
with extreme stylization.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:53.410
That’s probably where Cage’s tendency
to ad-lib made-up lyrics comes from, best

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:55.730
seen in this drunken interlude in Leaving
Las Vegas.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:59.390
“Like the Kling klang king of the rim ram
room.”

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:03.800
Gibb argues that what Cage calls his Western
Kabuki style and its appreciation for the

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:08.300
hyper-performative is the reason he tends
to adopt a different voice for every character

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:09.300
he plays.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:13.630
“I said, put the bunny back in the box”

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:19.730
For his own part, Cage says his vocal experimentation
has been an attempt to use his voice in almost

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:22.410
a heavy metal or operatic or baroque way.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.810
"Ever been dragged to the sidewalk and beaten
till you PISSED BLOOD?"

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:34.540
Indeed, it doesn’t get more heavy metal
than his screaming scenes in Mandy.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:39.810
[Screaming]

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:48.630
Cage’s insane attention to the details of
body movement is the reason his characters

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:51.890
in Adaptation feel so persuasively different.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:56.110
Cage plays twin brothers, who represent the
two sides of screenwriter Charlie Kaufman’s

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:57.110
personality.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:01.440
Charlie is a neurotic, chronic over thinker
obsessed with making genuine art, while Donald

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.520
is the charismatic Hollywood sellout.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:08.870
Fittingly, they move like two entirely different
people, an affect Cage says he created by

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.460
adjusting his spine.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:15.140
For Charlie’s rigid and controlled movements,
Cage hunched over and tensed up while he straightened

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:19.330
his back and loosened up to portray the more
go-with-the-flow Donald.

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:23.880
At other points, Cage draws inspiration from
German Expressionism, commonly viewed as the

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:28.740
first real film movement, which explored the
horrors of post world war I life in Germany

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:32.270
through highly-emotional, subjective storytelling.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:37.040
Dream-like horror films defined the genre,
depicting nightmarish worlds filled with twisted,

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.500
terrifying villains.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:42.950
These films left a lasting impact on the film
world, influencing genres like film noir,

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:45.980
horror and even graphic novels like Sin City.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.690
Nic Cage isn’t one to miss out on the fun.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:53.000
In interviews, he’s described watching classic
German Expressionist films like Nosferatu

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.890
and Metropolis as a kid, and described the
way certain distinct hand motions - like this

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:01.290
famous gesture in Moonstruck - were inspired
by those films.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:05.450
At other times, he’s modeled his characters’
physical attributes off of characters from

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.430
German Expressionist films.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:10.610
Other times, he’ll take things to a more
mystical levels.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:15.390
Take his role as Johnny Blaze in Ghost Rider,
a performance that was widely panned.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:20.820
Gibb describes how Cage adopted what he calls
“nouveau shamanism,” ”in which he integrated

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:26.360
Afro-Caribbean techniques” and sewed “thousand-year-old
Egyptian artifacts into his clothing.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:30.800
He also prepared by “painting his face white
and black like a voodoo priest.”

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:35.000
All of these techniques were meant trick him
into thinking he is a character from another

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.400
dimension with special powers.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:40.600
Keep in mind that this was all preparation
for this role.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.050
[sounds]

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:46.950
Rather than phoning in his superhero turn,
Cage decided to “push the boundaries of

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:47.950
what is comfortable.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:52.770
For his Oscar-winning performance in Leaving
Las Vegas, Cage studied cinema’s most convincing

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:57.060
alcoholics, and even hired a drunk actor to
hang out in his trailer.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.800
As a constant student of the craft, he takes
on roles not because they’ll improve his

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.380
reputation CLEARLY.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.280
[screams]

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:13.730
Instead, he tries radically new genres, roles
and pulls from new influences.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:18.540
Whether he’s channeling ancient vampires
or even more ancient shamans, Nicolas Cage

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:24.230
defiantly refuses to conform to realism, drawing
into question why that’s even the benchmark

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.260
for our understanding of good acting.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:30.330
We don’t fault the song “Yellow Submarine”
for unrealistically depicting the aquatic

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:31.330
life.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:35.520
Modernist authors like Virginia Woolf are
deemed revolutionary for the way their highly-stylized

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:41.140
novels depict life in the abstract, creating
unreliable timelines and dreamlike narratives.

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:45.450
Meanwhile, musicians like Arnold Schoenberg
and John Cage are considered geniuses for

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:48.400
breaking every rule of normal music-making.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:53.570
So why do we consider a departure from realism
to be an explicitly bad thing only when it

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:55.080
comes to acting?

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:59.920
Well we’ll venture a guess: Film, with its
close-ups and point of view shots, is arguably

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.020
the most intimate artform.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:06.900
When we so closely identify with a central
character, we naturally expect the performer

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:10.060
to behave approximately the way that we would.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:15.060
Part of why Nic Cage can be so maddening to
watch is he specifically refuses to do just

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.060
that.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.660
“I think you better pull the trigger…

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:20.000
Because I don’t give a [Beep]”

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.130
He’s also generally working with people
who don’t ascribe to his acting philosophy,

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.710
like if a death metal singer subbed in at
his local church choir.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:31.960
His wild reactions and bug-eyed expressions
confuse our expectations.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:36.880
They also make for particularly ridiculous
moments that, in a meme which erases the context

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:40.430
of film, make his performances seem ridiculous.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:43.950
But what if there are actual merits to his
out-there performance style?

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:48.140
Compare Nicholas Cage’s Peter in Vampire’s
Kiss to Christian Bale’s charismatic and

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:50.610
murderous Patrick Bateman in American Psycho.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:55.170
While both films are poking fun at the corporate
yuppy culture of the 80s, Bale’s Bateman

00:14:55.170 --> 00:15:01.390
has become an iconic antihero who is weirdly
revered and perversely admired for his highly-specific

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.430
brand of social-status obsessiveness.

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:06.080
“Look at that subtle off-white coloring.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:11.910
the tasteful thickness of it .Oh my god it
even has a watermark.”

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:18.540
In comparison, Cage’s Peter is so inaccessible,
so clearly performative, that he runs no risk

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.470
of becoming sympathetic to the audience.

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:22.820
“Ohhhhh”

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:30.730
Still, there’s a reason we’re talking
about Nic Cage and not, say, Chris Pine’s

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:34.120
acting style: It’s compelling, it’s different,
its controversial.

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.060
And ultimately, its subjective.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:41.000
You have every right to consider this ridiculous
or to consider it totally awesome.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:45.450
Either way, we’d argue that, with a few
more Nic Cage’s, Hollywood might just be

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:46.460
a lot more interesting.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:51.070
You don’t have to be president of your local
Nic Cage fan club to construe value in what

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.070
he’s doing.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.580
In fact, you can straight-up find him absurd.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.890
No matter what, you have to admit: He’s
pretty interesting.

